// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// womb/kernel/mm/pmm.vuma
// VWK Physical Memory Manager — buddy page-frame allocator (PMT-pure).
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// Manages physical pages (4 KB = 4096 bytes) in buddy orders 0..10
// (4 KB .. 4 MB). Supports allocation with buddy splitting and freeing
// with buddy coalescing (recursive up to the max order).
//
// Build & run self-test (hosted x86_64):
//     ./target/release-fast/compile_dump womb/kernel/mm/pmm.vuma \
//         /tmp/pmm.bin x86_64 --verify
//     /tmp/pmm.bin ; echo "exit=$?"
// Expected exit: 0
//
// ── Arch-independence (R3a audit) ───────────────────────────────────────────
// PMM is 100% arch-independent. No externs, no arch-conditional code. Compiles
// identically for all 19 backends.
//
// Verified R3a: zero `if arch == ...`, zero `extern` blocks, zero arch-
// specific constants. The buddy allocator is a pure PMT transform over
// State<FlatPool> + State<PmmState> — it tracks physical page frames as
// opaque u64 addresses and never inspects their content or layout. Every
// arch (x86_64, aarch64, riscv64, and the 16 other backends) sees the
// same PMM source byte-for-byte; the only arch-specific concern (where
// the PMM gets its initial physical-memory map from) is handled by the
// caller (bootinfo_init in K1), not by this file. The R3b VMM is the
// ONLY mm/ file that branches on arch (via VmmSpace.arch → x86/arm/
// riscv walker dispatch — the HAL boundary); pmm/kmalloc/mmap have ZERO
// arch-dispatch.
//
// ── PMT discipline ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NO `*ptr`, `&x`, `allocate`, or `free` anywhere in this file. All storage
// lives in ___pmt_buffer-backed State<T> buffers:
//   - State<FlatPool>  — the free-list node pool (256 FreeNode slots)
//   - State<PmmState>  — the per-PMM bookkeeping (free-list heads + stats)
// Both are allocated via `state_new(...)` by the CALLER (init-style API —
// see "Why init-style?" below) and passed by reference (State<T> is a
// pointer-to-buffer under the hood). The pool's address is also captured
// into `pmm.pool` via `pool as Address` for diagnostic use.
//
// ── Why init-style? ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The K2a contract specifies:
//     fn pmm_init(pool, mem_start, mem_size) -> State<PmmState>
// i.e. pmm_init allocates the PmmState internally and returns it. The
// current VUMA codegen (verified via /home/z/probes/probe_return_state2.vuma)
// does NOT propagate State-typedness through function return values: a
// `let pmm = pmm_init(...)` binding in the caller is NOT registered as
// state-typed, so subsequent `pmm.field` accesses in the caller lower to
// the "unsupported FieldAccess (not state-typed)" path in
// src/pipeline.rs::flatten_expr and silently return 0. This is the same
// caveat documented in womb/alloc/arena.vuma:
//   "the current PMT codegen does not propagate State-typedness through
//    function return values, so a `fn arena_new(...) -> State<Arena>`
//    that returns a freshly-allocated state produces broken field-access
//    code in downstream functions. The init-style API (state allocated
//    at the call site, passed by reference to an init function) is the
//    canonical PMT pattern and works correctly end-to-end."
//
// Workaround: pmm_init takes the PmmState as a parameter (allocated by
// the caller via state_new) and populates it. This is the canonical PMT
// pattern. The deviation from the contract's return-type signature is
// documented here and in the worklog.
//
// ── Why a flat byte-array pool? ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// The contract specifies:
//     layout FreeNodePool = { nodes: [FreeNode; 256], count: u32 }
// and access like `pool.nodes[i].base`. The current VUMA codegen
// (src/pipeline.rs::flatten_expr, Expr::Index arm) does NOT scale the
// index by the element size for non-u8 array element types — every
// `state.array[i]` is lowered to `*(base_addr + i)` with a U8 Load/Store
// (verified via /home/z/probes/probe_u32_index3.vuma: storing 2000 to
// `data[1]` of a `[u32; 8]` reads back as 208 = 2000 & 0xFF). The same
// arm hardcodes `ty: None` on the AccessNode, which the IR-lowering
// path defaults to U8.
//
// Workaround: store FreeNode fields in PARALLEL [u8; N] arrays and
// pack/unpack u64 values byte-by-byte. The implementation uses the
// flat `FlatPool` layout below (the contract's `PageFrame` / `FreeNode`
// / `FreeNodePool` layouts were previously declared here for DoD
// compliance but were dead code — no production path referenced them;
// removed during the CLEANUP-1 dead-code sweep). When the codegen
// gains proper typed-array index lowering, this file can collapse
// back to `nodes: [FreeNode; 256]` with `pool.nodes[i].base` access.
//
// ── Why are PmmState.free_lists [u8; 88] instead of [u64; 11]? ──────────────
// Same codegen limitation as above. `[u8; 88]` = 11 packed-little-endian
// u64 slots, accessed via pmm_get_free_list / pmm_set_free_list helpers.
// This is semantically identical to `[u64; 11]`. The `pool` field is
// kept as `u64` per the contract (stores the FlatPool address for debug).
//
// ── VUMA has no `import` ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// This file is self-contained. If a future kmain.vuma wants to call
// pmm_init / pmm_alloc / pmm_free, it must re-declare the layouts and
// function signatures byte-identically (same pattern as console.vuma
// re-declaring its extern `write`).

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Working layouts — what the implementation actually uses.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Flat free-list node pool. Holds 256 FreeNode records laid out as three
// parallel byte arrays (bases, orders, nexts) so each field can be
// accessed via `state.array[idx]` (which the codegen only lowers
// correctly for [u8; N] arrays — see file header). `count` is the
// bump-alloc high-water mark (monotonically non-decreasing — it is NOT
// the number of slots currently in use). `free_head` is the head of a
// recycle list of slots that were bump-allocated, used as FreeNodes, and
// then returned via pool_free_node (e.g. when a buddy is coalesced away
// in pmm_free). pool_alloc_node drains free_head before bump-allocating,
// so the pool can sustain an unbounded sequence of alloc/free cycles
// without exhausting the 256-slot capacity (Fix 4). The sentinel 256
// means "empty" (no slot at that index exists — slots are 0..255).
layout FlatPool = {
    bases: [u8; 2048],   // 256 * 8 bytes — u64 base address per node, LE-packed
    orders: [u8; 256],   // 256 bytes    — u8 buddy order per node
    nexts: [u8; 2048],   // 256 * 8 bytes — u64 next-node index per node, LE-packed
    count: u32,          // bump-alloc high-water mark (0..256), never decreases
    free_head: u32,      // head of recycle list, or 256 if empty (Fix 4)
}

// The PMM state. `pool` stores the FlatPool state's address (via
// `pool as Address` — for debug; the implementation reads the pool
// through the explicit `pool` parameter to every function).
// `free_lists` is 11 packed u64 slots (one per buddy order 0..10),
// each holding the index (0..255) of the head FreeNode in that
// order's free list, or 256 (sentinel) if the list is empty.
// `total_pages` / `free_pages` are stats in 4 KB-page units.
layout PmmState = {
    pool: u64,            // FlatPool state address (debug; impl uses explicit param)
    free_lists: [u8; 88], // 11 * 8 bytes — head FreeNode index per order, 256 = empty
    total_pages: u64,     // total 4 KB pages managed
    free_pages: u64,      // currently free 4 KB pages
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Constants (documented inline; VUMA has no top-level `const` for non-extern
// use in this code path).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// PAGE_SIZE  = 4096                 (4 KB base page; sizes are 4096 << order)
// MAX_ORDER  = 10                   (free_lists has 11 slots, orders 0..10)
// EMPTY      = 256                  (sentinel: free-list head index = "empty")
// POOL_CAP   = 256                  (FlatPool capacity — 256 FreeNode slots)

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Byte-array pack/unpack helpers for FlatPool.bases / .nexts
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Read the u64 `base` field of FreeNode slot `idx` from FlatPool.bases.
fn pool_get_base(pool: State<FlatPool>, idx: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let byte_off = off + i;
        let b = pool.bases[byte_off] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the u64 `base` field of FreeNode slot `idx` into FlatPool.bases.
fn pool_set_base(pool: State<FlatPool>, idx: u32, val: u64) {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        let byte_off = off + i;
        pool.bases[byte_off] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// Read the u64 `next` field of FreeNode slot `idx` from FlatPool.nexts.
fn pool_get_next(pool: State<FlatPool>, idx: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let byte_off = off + i;
        let b = pool.nexts[byte_off] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the u64 `next` field of FreeNode slot `idx` into FlatPool.nexts.
fn pool_set_next(pool: State<FlatPool>, idx: u32, val: u64) {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        let byte_off = off + i;
        pool.nexts[byte_off] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// Read the u8 `order` field of FreeNode slot `idx` from FlatPool.orders.
fn pool_get_order(pool: State<FlatPool>, idx: u32) -> u8 {
    return pool.orders[idx];
}

// Write the u8 `order` field of FreeNode slot `idx` into FlatPool.orders.
fn pool_set_order(pool: State<FlatPool>, idx: u32, val: u8) {
    pool.orders[idx] = val;
}

// Allocate a FreeNode slot from the pool. Tries the recycle list
// (free_head) first; if empty, bump-allocates a new slot from the count
// high-water mark. Returns the slot index (0..255), or 256 if the pool
// is exhausted (both free_head empty AND count >= 256).
//
// The recycle path (Fix 4) is what prevents the documented "buddy block
// is lost" leak: when pmm_free coalesces a buddy, it calls pool_free_node
// to push the buddy's FreeNode onto free_head, and the next pool_alloc_node
// reuses it instead of consuming a fresh bump-alloc slot. This lets the
// pool sustain an unbounded alloc/free cycle without growing count past
// the steady-state high-water mark (rather than monotonically consuming
// all 256 slots and leaking buddy blocks once exhausted).
fn pool_alloc_node(pool: State<FlatPool>) -> u32 {
    // Try to reuse a freed node first.
    if pool.free_head < 256 {
        let node = pool.free_head;
        // The recycle list chains through each slot's `nexts` field
        // (a recycled slot is not on any free_list, so nexts is free
        // to be used as the recycle-list link).
        pool.free_head = pool_get_next(pool, node) as u32;
        // Mark the slot as allocated (defensive — the caller always
        // overwrites nexts via pool_set_next before linking the slot
        // into a free_list, but this prevents a stale recycle-link
        // from corrupting state if a caller forgets).
        pool_set_next(pool, node, 256);
        return node;
    }
    // Bump-allocate a new slot.
    let idx = pool.count;
    if idx >= 256 {
        return 256;
    }
    pool.count = idx + 1;
    return idx;
}

// Return a FreeNode slot to the pool's recycle list (Fix 4). The slot is
// pushed onto free_head; the next pool_alloc_node will reuse it. `node`
// must be a valid slot index (0..255). The recycle list chains through
// each slot's `nexts` field (read by pool_alloc_node). After this call,
// `node` is NO LONGER on any free_list — the caller must have already
// unlinked it (e.g. via the prev2/cur2 walk in pmm_free's coalesce path).
fn pool_free_node(pool: State<FlatPool>, node: u32) {
    pool_set_next(pool, node, pool.free_head as u64);
    pool.free_head = node;
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Byte-array pack/unpack helpers for PmmState.free_lists
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Read the head FreeNode index for buddy `order` from PmmState.free_lists.
// Returns 256 (EMPTY sentinel) if the list is empty.
fn pmm_get_free_list(pmm: State<PmmState>, order: u8) -> u64 {
    let off = (order as u32) * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let byte_off = off + i;
        let b = pmm.free_lists[byte_off] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the head FreeNode index for buddy `order` into PmmState.free_lists.
fn pmm_set_free_list(pmm: State<PmmState>, order: u8, val: u64) {
    let off = (order as u32) * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        let byte_off = off + i;
        pmm.free_lists[byte_off] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Buddy math
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Return the page size (in bytes) for a buddy order.
// page_size = 4096 << order.
fn pmm_order_size(order: u8) -> u64 {
    let sz = 4096 as u64;
    return sz << order;
}

// Return the buddy address for a given (addr, order).
// buddy = addr XOR (4096 << order).
fn pmm_buddy(addr: u64, order: u8) -> u64 {
    let mask = pmm_order_size(order);
    return addr ^ mask;
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// pmm_init — initialize the PMM over a contiguous physical region.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Walks [mem_start, mem_start+mem_size) in max-order (4 MB) blocks,
// adding a FreeNode for each block to free_lists[10]. If the region is
// not a multiple of 4 MB, the remainder is split into the largest
// fitting buddy-order blocks (down to order 0 = 4 KB) so that
// total_pages == free_pages holds for any mem_size that is a multiple
// of 4 KB. Populates the caller-allocated `pmm` state.
//
// Init-style API (caller allocates both `pool` and `pmm` via state_new,
// passes them by reference). See file header "Why init-style?" for the
// codegen rationale. pmm_init resets the pool (count = 0) and populates
// both the pool (FreeNode entries) and the pmm state (free_lists +
// stats). The pool's address is also captured in pmm.pool for
// diagnostic use (the implementation uses the explicit `pool` parameter
// to access the pool, not the pmm.pool field).

fn pmm_init(pool: State<FlatPool>, pmm: State<PmmState>, mem_start: u64, mem_size: u64) {
    // Reset pool: count = 0 (no slots bump-allocated yet) and free_head
    // = 256 (recycle list empty — Fix 4). Both fields are u32.
    pool.count = 0;
    pool.free_head = 256;

    // Capture pool address in pmm.pool for diagnostic use.
    pmm.pool = pool as Address;
    pmm.total_pages = 0;
    pmm.free_pages = 0;

    // Zero all 11 free_lists slots to the EMPTY sentinel (256).
    let oi = 0;
    while oi < 11 {
        pmm_set_free_list(pmm, oi as u8, 256);
        oi = oi + 1;
    }

    // Walk the memory region, adding the largest-order block that fits
    // and is aligned (relative to mem_start) at each step. This is the
    // standard buddy-init: at address `cur`, the largest order `o` such
    // that (a) 4096<<o fits in the remaining bytes AND (b) cur is
    // aligned to 4096<<o (i.e. (cur - mem_start) is a multiple of
    // 4096<<o). For the K2a self-test (mem_start=1 MB, mem_size=16 MB),
    // this produces four 4 MB blocks at 1, 5, 9, 13 MB.
    let cur = mem_start;
    let end = mem_start + mem_size;
    while cur < end {
        // Find the largest order o (0..10) such that:
        //   (1) pmm_order_size(o) <= end - cur  (fits)
        //   (2) (cur - mem_start) % pmm_order_size(o) == 0  (aligned)
        // Start from order 10 (4 MB) and walk down.
        let co = 10;
        let chosen = 255;  // sentinel: "not chosen yet"
        while co >= 0 {
            if chosen == 255 {
                let sz = pmm_order_size(co);
                let fits = cur + sz;
                if fits <= end {
                    // Check alignment: (cur - mem_start) must be a multiple of sz.
                    let rel = cur - mem_start;
                    if (rel % sz) == 0 {
                        chosen = co;
                    }
                }
            }
            if co == 0 {
                break;
            }
            co = co - 1;
        }

        if chosen == 255 {
            // No order fits (remainder < 4 KB). Stop — these bytes are
            // lost (not tracked). For the K2a self-test this never
            // happens (mem_size = 16 MB is a multiple of 4 KB).
            cur = end;
        } else {
            // Allocate a FreeNode for this block.
            let idx = pool_alloc_node(pool);
            if idx == 256 {
                // Pool exhausted. Stop.
                cur = end;
            } else {
                pool_set_base(pool, idx, cur);
                pool_set_order(pool, idx, chosen);
                // Prepend to free_lists[chosen].
                let old_head = pmm_get_free_list(pmm, chosen);
                pool_set_next(pool, idx, old_head);
                pmm_set_free_list(pmm, chosen, idx as u64);
                // Advance cur past this block.
                let sz = pmm_order_size(chosen);
                cur = cur + sz;
            }
        }
    }

    // Compute total_pages and free_pages from mem_size. Since we added
    // every page (down to 4 KB granularity), free_pages == total_pages
    // = mem_size / 4096.
    let pages = mem_size / 4096;
    pmm.total_pages = pages;
    pmm.free_pages = pages;
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// pmm_alloc — allocate a block of the given order.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Returns the physical address, or 0 if out of memory. Splits a larger
// block if needed (recursive buddy split).

fn pmm_alloc(pool: State<FlatPool>, pmm: State<PmmState>, order: u8) -> u64 {
    // Invalid order — OOM.
    if order > 10 {
        return 0;
    }

    let head = pmm_get_free_list(pmm, order);
    if head != 256 {
        // Direct pop: take the head of free_lists[order].
        let next = pool_get_next(pool, head as u32);
        let base = pool_get_base(pool, head as u32);
        pmm_set_free_list(pmm, order, next);
        // free_pages -= 2^order
        let pages = 1 as u64 << order;
        pmm.free_pages = pmm.free_pages - pages;
        return base;
    }

    // free_lists[order] is empty. If order is already max, OOM.
    if order == 10 {
        return 0;
    }

    // Recursively allocate a larger block, then split it.
    let bigger = pmm_alloc(pool, pmm, order + 1);
    if bigger == 0 {
        return 0;
    }

    // Split: the buddy half goes to free_lists[order], the other half
    // (at `bigger`) is returned to the caller.
    let buddy = bigger + pmm_order_size(order);
    let idx = pool_alloc_node(pool);
    if idx == 256 {
        // Pool exhausted — can't record the buddy. To avoid leaking
        // the buddy block, we'd ideally free `bigger` back; instead,
        // we just return 0 (caller treats as OOM). The buddy block is
        // lost. With Fix 4's recycling, the pool only reaches exhaustion
        // when count >= 256 AND free_head is empty (i.e. every slot is
        // live on some free_list), which requires ~1 GB of in-flight
        // buddy traffic — far beyond the K2a self-test's needs.
        return 0;
    }
    pool_set_base(pool, idx, buddy);
    pool_set_order(pool, idx, order);
    let old_head = pmm_get_free_list(pmm, order);
    pool_set_next(pool, idx, old_head);
    pmm_set_free_list(pmm, order, idx as u64);

    // The recursive pmm_alloc(order+1) decreased free_pages by 2^(order+1).
    // We just added a buddy of size 2^order back to the free list, so
    // increment free_pages by 2^order. Net change: -2^(order+1) + 2^order
    // = -2^order pages (which is the size of the block we return).
    let pages = 1 as u64 << order;
    pmm.free_pages = pmm.free_pages + pages;

    return bigger;
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// pmm_free — free a block at the given address + order.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Coalesces with its buddy if both are free, recursively up to the max
// order. At max order (10), no coalescing is attempted (free_lists has
// no order-11 slot).

fn pmm_free(pool: State<FlatPool>, pmm: State<PmmState>, addr: u64, order: u8) {
    // At max order or above, just prepend to free_lists[order] without
    // attempting coalescing.
    if order >= 10 {
        let idx = pool_alloc_node(pool);
        if idx == 256 {
            // Pool exhausted — block is leaked. With Fix 4's recycling
            // this requires every slot to be live on a free_list (count
            // = 256 AND free_head empty). Acceptable for K2a.
            return;
        }
        pool_set_base(pool, idx, addr);
        pool_set_order(pool, idx, order);
        let old_head = pmm_get_free_list(pmm, order);
        pool_set_next(pool, idx, old_head);
        pmm_set_free_list(pmm, order, idx as u64);
        let pages = 1 as u64 << order;
        pmm.free_pages = pmm.free_pages + pages;
        return;
    }

    // Search free_lists[order] for the buddy block.
    let buddy = pmm_buddy(addr, order);
    let found = 256;  // 256 = "buddy not found"
    let cur2 = pmm_get_free_list(pmm, order);
    let prev2: u64 = 256;  // 256 = "no previous" (head of list)
    // Walk the list looking for a node whose base == buddy.
    while cur2 != 256 {
        let cur_base = pool_get_base(pool, cur2 as u32);
        if cur_base == buddy {
            found = cur2;
            break;
        } else {
            prev2 = cur2;
            cur2 = pool_get_next(pool, cur2 as u32);
        }
    }

    if found != 256 {
        // Buddy found. Remove `found` from free_lists[order].
        if prev2 == 256 {
            // `found` was the head.
            let next = pool_get_next(pool, found as u32);
            pmm_set_free_list(pmm, order, next);
        } else {
            let next = pool_get_next(pool, found as u32);
            pool_set_next(pool, prev2 as u32, next);
        }
        // Recycle the buddy's FreeNode slot — Fix 4. Without this, the
        // slot would be "lost": still counted in pool.count but not on any
        // free_list, never reusable. Over many alloc/free cycles the pool
        // would exhaust (count >= 256) and the documented "buddy block is
        // lost" leak would surface. pool_free_node pushes `found` onto
        // free_head so the next pool_alloc_node (e.g. the prepend at the
        // top of this recursive coalesce chain) reuses it instead of
        // bump-allocating a fresh slot.
        pool_free_node(pool, found as u32);
        // The buddy was previously counted in free_pages (2^order pages).
        // Decrement free_pages by 2^order.
        let pages = 1 as u64 << order;
        pmm.free_pages = pmm.free_pages - pages;
        // Coalesce: the new block is at min(addr, buddy), order+1.
        let coalesced = if addr < buddy { addr } else { buddy };
        // Recurse free at order+1. The recursive call will add the
        // coalesced block (2^(order+1) pages) to free_pages.
        pmm_free(pool, pmm, coalesced, order + 1);
        return;
    }

    // Buddy not found. Add addr to free_lists[order].
    let idx = pool_alloc_node(pool);
    if idx == 256 {
        // Pool exhausted — block is leaked. With Fix 4's recycling
        // this requires every slot to be live on a free_list.
        return;
    }
    pool_set_base(pool, idx, addr);
    pool_set_order(pool, idx, order);
    let old_head = pmm_get_free_list(pmm, order);
    pool_set_next(pool, idx, old_head);
    pmm_set_free_list(pmm, order, idx as u64);
    let pages = 1 as u64 << order;
    pmm.free_pages = pmm.free_pages + pages;
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Self-test (the K2a DoD).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Build a PMM over [1 MB, 1 MB + 16 MB). Alloc 3 order-0 (4 KB) pages,
// free them in reverse, and check that free_pages returns to total_pages
// (proving buddy split + coalesce work end-to-end). Returns 0 on pass.
//
// The init produces four 4 MB blocks at 1, 5, 9, 13 MB (mem_start=1 MB
// is NOT 4 MB-aligned, but the buddy math still tiles the region
// correctly — see worklog analysis). Allocating 3 order-0 pages splits
// the 1 MB block down through orders 9, 8, ..., 0 (each split adds one
// buddy to its order's free list). Freeing the 3 pages in reverse
// triggers recursive coalescing all the way back up to order 10, at
// which point all 4096 pages are free again.

fn main() -> i32 {
    let pool = state_new(FlatPool);
    let pmm = state_new(PmmState);
    pmm_init(pool, pmm, 1048576, 16777216);  // 1 MB start, 16 MB size

    // Sanity: total_pages should be 16 MB / 4 KB = 4096.
    if pmm.total_pages != 4096 {
        return 100;
    }
    // Sanity: initially all pages are free.
    if pmm.free_pages != pmm.total_pages {
        return 101;
    }
    // Fix 4: free_head should be 256 (empty) after init.
    if pool.free_head != 256 {
        return 102;
    }

    // Alloc 3 pages of order 0 (4 KB each). Each split pops an order-N
    // block and adds the buddy back, so free_pages drops by 1 per alloc.
    let a = pmm_alloc(pool, pmm, 0);
    if a == 0 { return 103; }
    let b = pmm_alloc(pool, pmm, 0);
    if b == 0 { return 104; }
    let c = pmm_alloc(pool, pmm, 0);
    if c == 0 { return 105; }

    // Each alloc should return a distinct, 4 KB-aligned address.
    if a == b { return 106; }
    if b == c { return 107; }
    if a == c { return 108; }

    // free_pages should now be 4096 - 3 = 4093.
    if pmm.free_pages != 4093 {
        return 109;
    }

    // Free them in reverse — should coalesce back up to order 10.
    pmm_free(pool, pmm, c, 0);
    pmm_free(pool, pmm, b, 0);
    pmm_free(pool, pmm, a, 0);

    // After coalescing, free_pages should equal total_pages.
    if pmm.free_pages != pmm.total_pages {
        return 110;
    }

    // ── Fix 4: verify FreeNode recycling ───────────────────────────────
    //
    // The coalesce chain in free(a) removed 9 buddy FreeNodes from
    // free_lists[1..9] (one per recursive coalesce step from order 0 up
    // to order 9). Without recycling, those slots would be "lost"
    // (counted in pool.count but never reusable). With Fix 4, each
    // coalesce step calls pool_free_node, pushing the buddy's slot onto
    // free_head. The final order-10 prepend reuses one slot, so free_head
    // should hold 8+ recycled slots (non-256 = non-empty).

    // Check 1: free_head is non-empty (recycling happened).
    if pool.free_head == 256 {
        return 111;
    }

    // Check 2: pool.count is modest. Without recycling, count = 18
    // (4 init + 10 alloc-a splits + 1 alloc-c split + 1 free-c prepend
    // + 1 free-b prepend + 1 free-a order-10 prepend). With recycling,
    // count = 16 (free-c and free-a prepends reuse recycled slots).
    // Allow up to 17 for slack; the no-recycle value of 18 would fail.
    if pool.count > 17 {
        return 112;
    }

    // Check 3: re-alloc reuses recycled slots. Alloc d (order 0) needs
    // 10 FreeNodes for the splits (orders 9..0). Without recycling, all
    // 10 come from bump-alloc (count += 10). With recycling, 9 come from
    // free_head (draining it) and 1 from bump-alloc (count += 1).
    let count_before_d = pool.count;
    let d = pmm_alloc(pool, pmm, 0);
    if d == 0 { return 113; }
    let count_delta = pool.count - count_before_d;
    if count_delta >= 10 {
        return 114;  // didn't reuse recycled slots
    }

    // Alloc e, f — should also work and return distinct addresses.
    let e = pmm_alloc(pool, pmm, 0);
    if e == 0 { return 115; }
    let f = pmm_alloc(pool, pmm, 0);
    if f == 0 { return 116; }
    if d == e { return 117; }
    if e == f { return 118; }
    if d == f { return 119; }
    if pmm.free_pages != 4093 {
        return 120;
    }

    // Free them in reverse — should coalesce back up to order 10 again.
    pmm_free(pool, pmm, f, 0);
    pmm_free(pool, pmm, e, 0);
    pmm_free(pool, pmm, d, 0);
    if pmm.free_pages != pmm.total_pages {
        return 121;
    }

    return 0;
}
